   802	## §8 Discussion
   803	
   804	- "Is decision-intelligence learnable?" — partially yes (Hartmann
   805	  suggestive; flat families no signal). At the distillation level: the
   806	  noise-dominated counterfactual teacher is still learnable at decision
   807	  level (0.40 → 0.87 pairwise; proper-score arm uniquely calibrated,
   808	  §5.6) — but the learned policy collapses to the teacher's constant
   809	  component in closed loop.
   810	- "Does it transfer across problems?" — see §4 RQ2 below.
   811	  Cross-family has lower regret vs real RulePolicy on 3/5 (Branin, Ackley,
   812	  Rastrigin) and vs AlwaysSurrogate on 2/5 (Ackley, Rastrigin); higher regret
   813	  vs AlwaysSurrogate on Hartmann + SixHumpCamel; inconclusive on Branin.
   814	- "Is LLM-as-System-1 worth it?" — against AlwaysSurrogate, only on hard
   815	  multimodal problems (Rastrigin +17.54, p=0.0058). But in closed loop
   816	  against a frozen RF System-1 (§6.5), the always-LLM policy never wins
   817	  with a CI excluding zero and loses detectably on 3/5 families at ~12–17
   818	  calls per trajectory. The LLM's positive value on this panel is confined
   819	  to the one-step comparison against weaker baselines.
   820	- "Is routing a Pareto win?" — measured (§6.5): no. No confidence threshold
   821	  or random rate earns its cost over System-1-only; the frontier is
   822	  degenerate on this panel. Whether any regime gives routing positive
   823	  value is the open question.
   824	- "Does the trained System-1 transfer across problems?" — see §4 RQ2 below
   825	  (wins vs real RulePolicy on 3/5; wins vs AlwaysSurrogate on 2/5; loses to a
   826	  constant local policy on aggregate). The RLCD fine-tuned model transfers
   827	  at decision level (+0.140, 5/5 folds, analysis-rerun; GearTrain probe
   828	  holds, §4.7) — closed-loop transfer remains undemonstrated for both.
   829	
   830	## §9 Conclusion
   831	
   832	- Three contributions: (1) a 7-component decision-centric BO framework
   833	  with **423 unit tests** (385 at first draft; +4 GearTrain; +2 partial-rollout
   834	  validation / Gate-3 CI-branch coverage in the 2026-09-28 audit round)
   835	  and four formal propositions tied to the experiments (`docs/THEORY.md`:
   836	  affine label invariance, adaptive-value bound, routing degeneracy,
   837	  exact imitation of axis-aligned rules); (2) a counterfactual oracle
   838	  dataset validation methodology that closes
   839	  Gate 3 (PASS at 20 seeds, adjusted_lift CI [0.848, 1.954]) and Gate 4
   840	  stop-gain (5/5), with **Gate 2 still failing** (5/15 buckets pass at
   841	  top_action_share ≥ 0.6) and **Gate 5 partial** (median Spearman = 1.0
   842	  necessarily by P1; predeclared joint fails on 2/17 buckets); (3) a
   843	  global-to-local temporal action pattern whose *sufficiency* is now
   844	  demonstrated: a deterministic two-phase schedule reproduces and exceeds
   845	  the LLM's closed-loop value on this panel.
   846	- **RQ2 (NEW)**: cross-family transfer **wins vs the real state-conditional
   847	  RulePolicy on 3/5 BOCoDe families** (Branin, Ackley, Rastrigin; seed-block
   848	  95% CI upper < 0). It also wins vs AlwaysSurrogate on 2/5 (Ackley,
   849	  Rastrigin). Aggregate Cross − Rule = -0.065 (cross is better). The cross
   850	  signal is heterogeneous (loses on Hartmann, SixHumpCamel vs Surrogate);
   851	  family × dimension confounded.
   852	- **RQ3 (cost frontier, NEW)**: in closed loop at 10 paired seeds the
   853	  frozen RF System-1 (0 LLM calls) beats or ties every LLM-calling arm on
   854	  all five families; always-LLM is CI-detectably worse on Ackley, Hartmann,
   855	  and SixHumpCamel. No confidence-threshold or random-rate router earns
   856	  its cost. The measured frontier is degenerate: System-1 only.
   857	- **EXP-RLCD (NEW 2026-09-28)**: decision-level distillation of the
   858	  counterfactual teacher into a 0.4B typed-decision model (pairwise
