   581	All p-values use Student-t distribution with df=n-1 (n=10 paired seeds).
   582	Source: `scripts/exp_rq0_5_proper_stats.py`. The early-report
   583	normal-approximation values were **stale** and inflated significance
   584	(e.g., Branin and Hartmann were reported as p<0.0001; the corrected
   585	Student-t values are 0.0012 and 0.0007 respectively).
   586	
   587	### §6.2 Mechanism: global-to-local temporal action pattern (correlational)
   588	
   589	From `runs/exp_rq0_5_action_distribution.md` — On Rastrigin (10D multimodal),
   590	the LLM exhibits a two-phase temporal pattern: predominantly `global_exploration`
   591	at steps 0–9 (90–100% across these steps; 1/10 trajectories spike to
   592	`local_exploitation` at steps 3, 6, 8, 9); mixed at steps 10–12 (30–40% global,
   593	60–80% local); 100% `local_exploitation` from step 13 onward. AlwaysSurrogate
   594	picks `surrogate_acquisition` 100% of all 25 steps.
   595	
   596	**Important caveats**:
   597	- The earlier §5 of `EXP_RQ1_3_REPORT.md` hypothesized the LLM might be picking
   598	  `local_exploitation` (the closed-loop best first action) — that hypothesis is
   599	  **rejected** by the per-step distribution (LLM picks `global_exploration` 70%
   600	  on Rastrigin, not `local_exploitation`).
   601	- The LLM's **pooled** top action on Rastrigin is `global_exploration` at 70%,
   602	  which is **similar to its share on smoother families** (Branin 76%, Hartmann
   603	  67%, Ackley 66%, SixHumpCamel 68%) — *families where LLM does NOT beat
   604	  Surrogate*. The pooled distribution alone does not explain the Rastrigin-
   605	  specific advantage; only the *temporal pattern* is unique.
   606	- **Deterministic schedule control now run (NEW)**: the predeclared
   607	  `GlobalThenLocal` baseline (10 global decisions, then local) was run on
   608	  the exact RQ3 panel (seeds 100–109, paired starts verified). It **beats
   609	  the real LLM with CI excluding zero on Hartmann (+0.334), SixHumpCamel
   610	  (+0.674), and Rastrigin (+9.628)** and never loses to it; it ties the
   611	  frozen RF on three families (RF keeps Ackley and SixHumpCamel).
   612	  On Rastrigin the ordering is schedule ≈ rf > llm, which **subsumes** the
   613	  earlier "LLM > AlwaysSurrogate on Rastrigin" pilot: the schedule alone
   614	  reproduces and exceeds the LLM's value. See §6.5.
   615	- **Recommended paper wording (updated per codex design adjudication
   616	  2026-09-28)**: *"On Rastrigin, the LLM exhibited a global-to-local
   617	  temporal action pattern associated with its advantage over
   618	  AlwaysSurrogate. A deterministic two-phase schedule realizing that
   619	  pattern matches the LLM's value and exceeds it on three of five
   620	  families — a two-phase schedule suffices to match or exceed the LLM's
   621	  value on this panel, so LLM-specific per-step reasoning is not needed
   622	  to explain it."*
   623	
   624	### §6.3 FrozenRouter: BOCoDe advantage does NOT transfer
   625	
   626	| Panel | FrozenRouter − Surrogate | p |
   627	|---|---|---|
   628	| BOCoDe (5-family) | +15.69 (sum) | 0.012 |
   629	| IOH (f1–f5, dim 2/5/10/20) | +0.036 (mean normalized regret) | 0.138 |
   630	
   631	**Negative result**: a simple `dim≥5` router trained on BOCoDe does not
   632	generalize to IOH/BBOB at the same scale.
   633	
   634	### §6.4 RoutingAware vs LLM-everywhere (negative finding)
   635	
   636	On the BOCoDe panel sum, RoutingAware − LLM = +1.18 (paired-t p=**0.5134**,
   637	not significant). The simple dimension-based routing rule is NOT measurably
   638	better than LLM-everywhere on this benchmark. **The Pareto frontier
   639	deferral**: more elaborate routing needs RQ2 transfer experiments to
   640	become a defensible claim.
   641	
   642	### §6.5 Real-LLM cost frontier, closed loop, 10 seeds (EXP-RQ3)
   643	
   644	Frozen RF System-1 (trained on rollout seeds 0–15, temperature-calibrated on
   645	16–19) deployed through full budget-30 BO trajectories against the same
   646	`deepseek-chat` System-2, with confidence-threshold routers (τ ∈ {0.25,
   647	0.5, 0.75, 0.9}) and random-rate baselines (rate ∈ {0.25, 0.5, 0.75}).
   648	**450 paired trajectories** (5 families × seeds 100–109 × 9 policies),
   649	identical Sobol starts per (family, seed), zero LLM fallbacks, identical
   650	frozen-model hash across both sweep files.
   651	
   652	| Family | `rf − llm` (mean) | `llm − rf` 95 % CI | verdict |
   653	|---|---:|---|---|
   654	| Branin | +0.515 | [-1.224, +0.152] | rf better in mean, CI crosses 0 |
   655	| Hartmann | +0.576 | **[-1.032, -0.103]** | rf detectably better |
   656	| Ackley | +1.017 | **[-1.658, -0.436]** | rf detectably better |
   657	| SixHumpCamel | +1.015 | **[-1.512, -0.587]** | rf detectably better |
   658	| Rastrigin | +10.109 | [-22.356, +0.404] | rf better in mean, CI crosses 0 |
   659	
   660	**No arm on the whole grid (8 LLM-calling arms × 5 families) beats `rf`
   661	with a CI excluding zero**; the only positive point estimates anywhere are
   662	Hartmann/confidence_0.25 (+0.018) and Hartmann/random_0.25 (+0.149), both
   663	CI-crossing. Always-LLM spends ~12–17 calls (~4.7–6.5k tokens) per
   664	trajectory and is dominated. The pre-specified acceptance criteria (≥ 50 %
   665	call/token reduction at ≤ 0.10 σ_family regret margin) pass trivially via
   666	the System-1-only arm — the measured frontier is degenerate.
   667	
   668	**Sign correction (documentation integrity).** The first 3-seed version of
   669	this experiment inverted the sign of its own Hartmann numbers in prose
   670	("the one family where the LLM is genuinely useful"); the recorded data had
   671	`rf` winning 3/0/0. The corrected reading agrees with §6.1 above (LLM
   672	−0.985 on Hartmann, p=0.0007). See the correction block at the top of
   673	`runs/EXP_RQ3_REAL_LLM_COST_FRONTIER_REPORT.md`.
   674	
   675	**Routing diagnostic.** The 3-seed "random rate beats the confidence
   676	router" observation does not generalize: at 10 seeds neither scheme
   677	produces a CI-detectable gain over `rf`, and Branin random_0.5/0.75 are
   678	CI-detectably worse. The RF softmax maximum rarely exceeds 0.25, so
   679	confidence thresholds route nearly every step to System-2;
   680	`ConfidenceRouter.tau_conf` is not a useful routing signal on this panel.
   681	
   682	**Schedule control (mechanism).** A predeclared deterministic
   683	`GlobalThenLocal` control (10 global decisions, then local) run on this
   684	exact panel (paired starts verified) **beats the LLM arm with CI excluding
   685	zero on Hartmann (+0.334), SixHumpCamel (+0.674), and Rastrigin (+9.628)**
   686	and never loses to it; it ties `rf` on Branin/Hartmann/Rastrigin while RF
   687	keeps Ackley (−0.853) and SixHumpCamel (−0.341). The LLM's closed-loop
   688	decision content is dominated by the fixed two-phase schedule its own
   689	per-step distribution exhibits — none of the tested routers earned its
   690	cost, consistent with THEORY.md P3 (IF an arm's per-state advantage is
   691	≤ 0, no router can rescue it; our data are consistent with, but do not
   692	independently establish, that premise). Artifacts:
   693	`runs/exp_rq3_schedule_baseline.json`,
   694	`scripts/exp_rq3_schedule_baseline.py`.
   695	

